<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Romanos I Lekapenos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 870–<a href="/years/948">948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Romanus I Lecapenus · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty">Macedonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Romanos I Lekapenos was Byzantine emperor from 920 to 944. An admiral of humble origin, he became father-in-law and co-emperor of the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> and governed in his place. He made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 927, supported the eastern offensives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-kourkouas">John Kourkouas</a> and passed laws protecting small landholders. His sons deposed him in 944, and he died as a monk.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romanos-i-lekapenos.jpg" alt="Romanos I Lekapenos" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dumbarton Oaks · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Romanos_I_Lekapenos.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Romanos took his epithet from his birthplace, Lakape, between Melitene and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samosata">Samosata</a>. His father was a peasant, Theophylaktos, who had saved the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a> in battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divrigi">Tephrike</a> in 872 and was rewarded with a place in the Imperial Guard and estates. Romanos had no refined education, for which his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> later abused him, but he rose in the army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-vi-the-wise">Leo VI the Wise</a>. He was general of the naval theme of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> in 911 and later admiral of the fleet. After the Bulgarian victory at the Battle of Acheloos in 917, he sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and gradually overcame the discredited regency of Empress Zoe Karvounopsina.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 25 March 919, at the head of his fleet, Romanos seized the Boukoleon Palace and the government. In April his daughter Helena married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a>, and he took the title basileopator. He was named caesar on 24 September 920 and crowned senior emperor on 17 December. He crowned his own sons co-emperors, Christopher in 921 and Stephen and Constantine in 924, but left Constantine VII untouched, and so was called the gentle usurper.</p><p>The war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-i-of-bulgaria">Simeon I of Bulgaria</a> took the first four years of his reign. In 924 he met Simeon at Kosmidion and ended the siege of the capital, in effect by tacitly recognising him as emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. After Simeon's death in May 927 his granddaughter Maria, renamed Eirene, married the new Bulgarian emperor Peter I in September.</p><p>In the east Romanos' general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-kourkouas">John Kourkouas</a> campaigned from 926 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> and won Melitene in 934, often seen as the first major Byzantine territorial recovery from the Muslims. In 944 his withdrawal from Edessa gained the relic called the mandylion. A Kievan raid in 941 was defeated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-fire">Greek fire</a>, and in 944 Romanos made a treaty with Prince Igor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. At home he issued laws protecting small landowners from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dynatoi">dynatoi</a>, increased taxes on the aristocracy and, in 933, made his young son Theophylaktos patriarch.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Romanos grew guilt-ridden over the usurpation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a>'s throne. His eldest son Christopher died in 931. Fearing that Romanos would let Constantine VII succeed, his younger sons Stephen and Constantine arrested him on 20 (or 16) December 944 and forced him to become a monk on the Princes' Islands.</p><p>The people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> rose when the sons threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a>, and the sons were also exiled. Romanos died on 15 June 948 and was buried in the church of Myrelaion, the burial church he had built beside his palace. Constantine VII wrote that Romanos was an uneducated man of low descent, and his descendants included every emperor for the next century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Romanos I Lekapenos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Any man the army, Senate and people accepted could rule, and outsiders did: Basil I, born to peasant parents, rose from groom to co-emperor and seized sole power in 867, and <b>Romanos I Lekapenos</b>, a peasant’s son and admiral, was crowned in 920.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Romanos I Lekapenos</b>, an admiral of humble origin and father-in-law of the young Constantine VII, was crowned co-emperor and governed in his place.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-17">17 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Byzantine emperor <b>Romanos I Lekapenos</b>, regent and senior co-ruler for Constantine VII since 920, was arrested by two of his sons and forced to abdicate.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-20">20 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>They were subdued by Theoktistos Bryennios in the 840s and rebelled again in the 920s under <b>Romanos I</b>, after which their tribute was raised.</blockquote><a href="/state/ezeritai">Ezeritai</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=920" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Romanos I Lekapenos"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4934 4064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4934 4064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=920">What was here in 920 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
